HST Office: .xlsx keeps formulas whose result is an error value; only a formula that did not compile is left out.

hst-sheets-excel-formula-conventions.patch leaves a formula that never compiled (LibreOffice keeps "=1+*2" and shows
an error) out of the .xlsx, because the leading application refuses the whole file. It tested every code error of the
formula, so formulas that are valid there were lost on save too and only their error value was written: an error
value in the formula (=#N/A, upstream testTdf131372) and a name that is not defined, such as an external defined name
without a value ([1]!HelloWorld, testEmptyExternalDefinedNames). Now only the compiler's syntax errors (invalid
character, missing bracket, operator or operand, parameter list, overflows, unknown token) leave the formula out.

License: these upstream files remain under their original license (MPL-2.0).
Test: make CppunitTest_sc_subsequent_export_test4 (testTdf131372, testEmptyExternalDefinedNames);
tests/runtime/check-sheets-excel-formula-conventions.py (=1+*2 is still left out).
---
--- a/sc/source/filter/excel/xetable.cxx
+++ b/sc/source/filter/excel/xetable.cxx
@@ -1039,7 +1039,31 @@ void XclExpFormulaCell::SaveXml( XclExpXmlStream& rStrm )
         // HST: a formula kept with a syntax error (it never compiled, e.g. "=1+*2") is not
         // written: the leading application refuses the whole file, also when asked to repair
         // it. The cell keeps its error result.
-        bool bValid = aTokenArray.GetCodeError() == FormulaError::NONE;
+        // Only a formula that did not compile: an error value in the formula (=#N/A) or a name that
+        // is not defined (#NAME?, [1]!Name) is a formula there too, and stays one.
+        bool bValid = true;
+        switch (aTokenArray.GetCodeError())
+        {
+            case FormulaError::IllegalChar:
+            case FormulaError::IllegalParameter:
+            case FormulaError::Pair:
+            case FormulaError::PairExpected:
+            case FormulaError::OperatorExpected:
+            case FormulaError::VariableExpected:
+            case FormulaError::ParameterExpected:
+            case FormulaError::CodeOverflow:
+            case FormulaError::StringOverflow:
+            case FormulaError::StackOverflow:
+            case FormulaError::UnknownState:
+            case FormulaError::UnknownVariable:
+            case FormulaError::UnknownOpCode:
+            case FormulaError::UnknownStackVariable:
+            case FormulaError::UnknownToken:
+                bValid = false;
+                break;
+            default:
+                break;
+        }
         // Discard formulas containing macros in XLSX export.
         if (!rStrm.IsExportVBA())
         {
